            is defined.
          %h3#mutual_inverses Mutual inverses
          %p
            %strong Theorem
            The previously defined 2-functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            are mutually (weakly) inverse 2-equivalences between the bicategory of semisimple
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-action," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-action." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_g
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \simeq (\mathcal{C}^G)_G
          %h3#forgetful_and_induction_functors Forgetful and induction functors
          %ul
            %li
              There is an obvious faithful forgetful functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon \mathcal{C}^G \to \mathcal{C}
            %li
              There is a left and right adjoint to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi IX
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊕
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi G
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IX = \oplus_{g \in G} \rho(g) X
            %li
              There is an obvious forgetful functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'\colon \mathcal{C}_G \to \mathcal{C}
            %li
              There is a left adjoint to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FX
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FX = k(G) \otimes X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h1#monoidal_and_braided_categories_and_central_functors Monoidal and braided categories, and central functors
          %p The previous constructions generalise easily when our categories acquire monoidal or braided structures.
          %p
            Actions of groups on monoidal categories are simply a monoidal equivalence for every group element with the same extra structure as mentioned before. Actions of groups on braided categories are additionally required to preserve the braiding. (Following the idea of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+and+properties"} stuff, structure, and properties
          %p
            The action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k-\operatorname{Vect}
            on a
            %em monoidal
            linear category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            can also be understood as the canonical monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k-\operatorname{Vect} \to \mathcal{C}
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            to the monoidal unit, followed by the tensor product. In this situation, the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G
            module structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            can then also be understood as the canonical functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                      %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G \to \mathcal{C}^G
          %p
            By abstract nonsense, an action on an algebraic object should be a morphism into its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            on a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            should be given by a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒵
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} \to \mathcal{Z}(\mathcal{C})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒵
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Z}
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Drinfeld+center"} Drinfeld center
            = succeed "." do
              %em central functor
            %em symmetric category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            on a braided category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            should be a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            is the “symmetric center”, or “Müger center”.
          %p
            The previously mentioned functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                    %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G \to \mathcal{C}^G
            factors through the forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒵
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Z}(\mathcal{C}) \to \mathcal{C}
            (or, in the case of a braided category, through the inclusion
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}' \hookrightarrow \mathcal{C}
          %p
            %strong Theorem
            By equivariantisation, the bicategory of fusion categories with
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G
          %h2#modularisation Modularisation
          %p
            Each braided fusion category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            has a canonical symmetric subcategory, its symmetric centre
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            has a canonical spherical structure. If all the quantum dimensions are positive, that is, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %em tannakian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Rep}_G
            for a unique group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (since there is a canonical fibre functor). The obvious construction is then to deequivariantise over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}' \simeq \operatorname{Rep}_G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_G
            %strong modularisation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
          %p There is an “exact sequence” of braided fusion categories:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Rep
                    %mi G
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ↠
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k-\operatorname{Vect} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{B}' \simeq \operatorname{Rep}_G \hookrightarrow \mathcal{B} \twoheadrightarrow \mathcal{B}_G
          %p
            It is exact in the sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            is the maximal symmetric subcategory of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_G
            is the maximal modular category such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}'
            is sent to sums of the monoidal unit.
          %h1#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Vladimir Drinfeld, Shlomo Gelaki, Dmitri Nikshych, Victor Ostrik,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On braided fusion categories I
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.0620"} arXiv
            %li
              Alain Bruguières,
              %em Catégories prémodulaires, modularisations et invariants des variétés de dimension 3
              (french)
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.univ-montp2.fr/~bruguieres/docs/modular.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              Michael Müger,
              %em Galois Theory for Braided Tensor Categories and the Modular Closure
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9812040"} arXiv
